#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "85acf106-2f03-5509-8ad0-08b82ef2a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8005" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058571/assets/9cb26049b5e3d06f9ef9e07ce617042166330b1eddb84063d7970799ebf6d8e5.png)\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of $a_{31}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of $a_{31}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-3){C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(11-3) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b0edd60-2801-59d3-b9df-18bfbf8763bb" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "4a9954b2-3d4b-5669-88cd-6e74c9683a60" :question/math-academy-id "e-8054" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries below $a_{11}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "ccf558b9-bf00-58c7-b244-2c5797f93d8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8060" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b0edd60-2801-59d3-b9df-18bfbf8763bb" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71d4e9d7-33e6-5927-b62a-755b481a809d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336878} {:answer/id #uuid "48165388-2d2c-50f7-9658-41d06f7b326e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336879} {:answer/id #uuid "61ed482f-8986-596d-8a88-8dd91787d90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336880} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5a7abe-3eef-5511-ba42-132fcd103f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 15 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336881} {:answer/id #uuid "4cba9579-ca6f-5558-ac8b-ba6b714678ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71d4e9d7-33e6-5927-b62a-755b481a809d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336878} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a2fe9ba-6d96-53c3-acd0-a67eb16b6183" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcd84b01-ed2a-54d5-a814-fd8e2737e1eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-131256" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{34}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{34}=3$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73a85f4d-6ce1-53bc-9c1a-dd39a8b334c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336885} {:answer/id #uuid "bc58d283-1459-57cd-9ade-e7a6f8b88412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336886} {:answer/id #uuid "59fa360c-9b19-5a09-93d9-e3b424a78a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5|\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 11\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336887} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3f1477-bf8f-5be3-915e-16c350d44ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336888} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6ece9a-0497-5a58-9a32-833f91e1801c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73a85f4d-6ce1-53bc-9c1a-dd39a8b334c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336885} :answer-field/id #uuid "745488a4-df38-5c6e-b714-a3d07738386e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a9dab18-4457-5979-bd0f-fac7f073a015" :question/math-academy-id "q-132024" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 10 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left of ${a}_{14}=3$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 10 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-3){C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 11 & -5 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 11 & -5 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8091471-fb90-557b-bb9e-8cdcfdbdaa76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336892} {:answer/id #uuid "5495067b-1029-50db-866f-f4605ecdb608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336893} {:answer/id #uuid "54379468-bff4-52ce-9743-8a635670383f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336894} {:answer/id #uuid "5e575ac8-d52e-547b-a645-b827ad41fab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336895} {:answer/id #uuid "b374b0eb-9504-5f09-8296-be9c533d5e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e575ac8-d52e-547b-a645-b827ad41fab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336895} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eab9cdf-e173-553b-812d-0f9f1a778f55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31e66c9-759f-52b2-8d86-9070451608b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132142" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -8 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{12}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -8 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -5 & -4 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -5 & -4 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -5 & -4 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -5 & -4 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot -5\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(-8+35) \\\\  & =135.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f62ff9-dca1-5597-a531-6202f13c697b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336899} {:answer/id #uuid "80abfa9b-0bc6-5a72-bffd-f546b318a434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336900} {:answer/id #uuid "92a34893-3e6e-552c-8bfe-aca7a6282c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "278" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336901} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ea8d63-274b-5730-8d3a-59930b9f567f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336902} {:answer/id #uuid "616b59bf-7c90-52b1-9718-799656892c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "312" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34f62ff9-dca1-5597-a531-6202f13c697b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336899} :answer-field/id #uuid "39716324-40b5-5742-b56e-7ae616ee4311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f73568a9-ff00-5c95-a522-dda148a52036" :question/math-academy-id "q-132144" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries above and below ${a}_{32}=3$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot -3\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9(30+6) \\\\  & =324.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "219049fa-15c0-565c-bb51-4ad687cc38f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "414" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336906} {:answer/id #uuid "8022e9df-6893-5d2c-a1e7-c005e27a312b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "378" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336907} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd493ee-05b8-560a-87f7-dc40dadcfb2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "298" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336908} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca79b3e-ccee-5a00-a019-1843e98337b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "531" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336909} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffbe4dc-0f2c-5b32-ad0c-fea3976140a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "462" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "219049fa-15c0-565c-bb51-4ad687cc38f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "414" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336906} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ad3bfd-67fa-5606-abe8-49971b346b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3183b669-59a5-59fd-b05c-573c33b432b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132163" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -8 & -2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{11}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{11}=3$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -8 & -2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 9 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 9 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (3)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}9 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9(18+28) \\\\  & =9(46) \\\\  & =414.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b0edd60-2801-59d3-b9df-18bfbf8763bb" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e851acc6-815c-5a3e-8b88-4fa8bf7c3c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336913} {:answer/id #uuid "75099e04-2ebe-52ed-bf4d-dc4db98e2f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 10 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336914} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cd7748-a651-5d43-bc6f-96dfe66b8d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336915} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfd7768-89e9-561f-864b-f8bdcfe336da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336916} {:answer/id #uuid "000fd2c2-5835-528e-b4aa-d7bb3daf1540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dfd7768-89e9-561f-864b-f8bdcfe336da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336916} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e5101b-dc4a-5350-a93c-a2e21dd0a1e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76b3cd6b-fbb2-5a67-a041-718320a952b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-42902" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{22}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37038714-9647-5ea0-a98e-c8c5ca5b9258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336919} {:answer/id #uuid "d035ede5-7dd3-54c2-8ad5-c1cf8eebd74c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336920} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe773f2-7fcd-51e4-b44c-faed28ea7de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336921} {:answer/id #uuid "c219a03b-bdb4-5df3-9a23-33dd1b5362a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336922} {:answer/id #uuid "a9460acc-759f-59ab-8ef2-b9ede7534741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37038714-9647-5ea0-a98e-c8c5ca5b9258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336919} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7229a2c-74af-5c08-879f-ff8447d08ee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ea53fce-52af-53d1-b17c-6cd66b5ff019" :question/math-academy-id "q-91092" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}=2$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-1){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b0edd60-2801-59d3-b9df-18bfbf8763bb" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf7fb21-4d39-5e94-8aa1-33d2f7bc8fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336925} {:answer/id #uuid "444d7a36-92b5-5dc7-9598-48e41bc1ac63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336926} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9895e0-af93-5b2e-972f-911321acf81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336927} {:answer/id #uuid "1957f911-7530-53dd-b2fd-eba5c74ce21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336928} {:answer/id #uuid "177ae27f-1b69-5f1a-9ce4-c2f67e3820a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "177ae27f-1b69-5f1a-9ce4-c2f67e3820a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336929} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d97adee-6651-53e5-8193-2da96df85b43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b50beb4-268d-5e94-a279-936396d00949" :question/math-academy-id "q-91166" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries below ${a}_{14}=3$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -11 & 4 & 10 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -11 & 4 & 10 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}|\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 & 10 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b0edd60-2801-59d3-b9df-18bfbf8763bb" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2d0a0e7-6c33-52ea-a67b-b6c874a0d4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336932} {:answer/id #uuid "0d05fa6c-05d7-5999-a595-5233cb55367f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 16\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336933} {:answer/id #uuid "37d8c8bf-57e9-5f84-859a-1f71afaf98c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336934} {:answer/id #uuid "b94465e2-29ca-56f2-b9b4-dafe7d7e5234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336935} {:answer/id #uuid "aac7db63-f304-54dc-86bf-03651972d48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aac7db63-f304-54dc-86bf-03651972d48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336936} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d7ddeb3-d17a-59df-b7b4-d9d3d3c49864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef6e2c2f-2598-5c84-a9f7-82fa31430417" :question/math-academy-id "q-91169" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{23}=-2$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 0 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 0 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b0edd60-2801-59d3-b9df-18bfbf8763bb" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cda0dba-47c9-5a90-b8de-e7bf60673df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336939} {:answer/id #uuid "507ec909-ab8f-5772-a282-8379e9439282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336940} {:answer/id #uuid "3bab72f6-011a-52cd-9648-a7018544cf3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 11 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336941} {:answer/id #uuid "fda3077b-1767-5153-815f-c4e1c50330aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336942} {:answer/id #uuid "0bf36e38-3d01-528e-99fe-1dc4ff1ab31e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cda0dba-47c9-5a90-b8de-e7bf60673df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336939} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bfbaf43-7110-5710-a9e0-71905bb598fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5692854a-1edd-56c8-8906-b5a68a5133f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-91170" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries below ${a}_{12}=-1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 & 11 \\\\ -6 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be9005ee-5494-52ab-8266-15c965d513db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336946} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc327eb-2e0f-5856-923b-879720eac8c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336947} {:answer/id #uuid "0adeb05a-3399-594c-9905-9001c279c0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218