end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}14 & -20 & 6 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & 7 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}14 & -20 & 6 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & 7 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}|\\begin{aligned}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c805bb3-4946-5767-91eb-69cd2ae6bbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336953} {:answer/id #uuid "a17c353f-1c66-5848-8236-dc3f7b03b3b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336954} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1b4389-df88-566b-9cfa-a33e317759b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336955} {:answer/id #uuid "1330083e-c51b-519b-a26e-7ada59c9301e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336956} {:answer/id #uuid "06d43315-2e07-5ef5-97ff-6537b6cacce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06d43315-2e07-5ef5-97ff-6537b6cacce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336957} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d392098-66a4-50be-bcfd-2eef5ca1ef36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ff3ecd8-fc32-55c0-acc0-bdc23d568e06" :question/math-academy-id "q-91217" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{42}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{42}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-2){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fddc869-efe8-53ee-9b0d-89ab336e3063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336960} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8fbf63-c761-52fb-8d32-069afb324476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336961} {:answer/id #uuid "d453a4e3-5d1a-562d-82f2-c4286d075639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336962} {:answer/id #uuid "39d189a2-7168-540f-8b00-ea4e38057bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336963} {:answer/id #uuid "a588ee1b-f967-5090-9d9e-0c88d8a2f3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8fbf63-c761-52fb-8d32-069afb324476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336961} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e05ab5d-32c7-5503-bdef-8a5bc423e9bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b6963e3-5249-5961-8869-b1c3b881e091" :question/math-academy-id "q-91218" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+3{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-4){C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d90982-0e20-59bc-a30a-726600dce87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336967} {:answer/id #uuid "a70f5b14-1e01-5eb1-a8a0-a6789300c0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336968} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f1eab1-6e0d-59be-b418-06d6b6ae0cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336969} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdee810-5a81-57a9-b9e0-774a49905a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336970} {:answer/id #uuid "1199db10-ded7-55d5-88e7-6df87244d76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1199db10-ded7-55d5-88e7-6df87244d76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336971} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e17c15e-feff-549b-b204-6d4b34f4110d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3736ca09-423c-5fd6-a72b-820ba0574201" :question/math-academy-id "q-91229" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{21}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-6-5) \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6789823-26ec-5ebc-a96b-4e310960ff89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336974} {:answer/id #uuid "be40011b-55d7-536f-8dea-1d30d74113b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336975} {:answer/id #uuid "1df06396-adf4-5c4a-863a-df96cecc57df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336976} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d8799b-1d29-5b44-8f0e-5bc7837d2929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336977} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba858ff-6303-557f-8584-806b0c8092d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be40011b-55d7-536f-8dea-1d30d74113b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336975} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb7b472-7759-529a-ba71-85151572d36f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1e3c7d7-0f45-570f-9572-185c6fec00c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-91232" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the left of ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(49-25) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
